--TEST--
Core\Time::fromIso refuses a text that names no instant, where strtotime() guesses one from ambient state or answers false
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`strtotime` is two members wearing one name. Half of it reads a timestamp that carries its own offset, and that half agrees with `Core\Time::fromIso` byte for byte — asserted against PHP itself in `time-from-iso-matches-strtotime-over-a-timestamp-that-carries-its-offset`. The other half *guesses*, and every one of its guesses reads state the text does not name: a civil time with no offset (`"2024-02-29"`, `"2024-02-29T13:45:07"`) is placed in whatever `date_default_timezone_set` last held, so the same input is two different instants in two deployments; a relative expression (`"+1 day"`, `"now"`, `"next Thursday"`) is resolved against the clock, so it is not a parse at all but an arithmetic whose other operand is the moment the call happened; and a text that spells nothing (`"not a date"`, `""`) answers `false`, which is `0` in every numeric position PHP will then put it in — the epoch, silently, one report later. Spec § 4 splits those three readings across three members, and `fromIso`'s one argument is text, so a text that does not spell a timestamp is the input's failure rather than the call site's: it is a `ParseError` (`nvs_stdlib::time`'s `nvs_core_time_from_iso`), catchable like any other `Throwable` (`rule:errors/propagation`), and never a value. Each row below is written twice — once as the refusal, and once as the member that says out loud what the `strtotime` call meant: `Core\Time::parse(text, pattern, zone)` names the zone the guess would have read from ambient state, and calendar arithmetic over an instant names the moment the relative expression would have counted from. `"2024-02-30T00:00:00Z"` is the fourth guess and the one that reaches a *valid* offset: PHP normalizes the day that does not exist into 2024-03-01, which is the same rollover `Core\Time::at` refuses and `time-at-throws-where-mktime-rolls-a-date-over` owns. The one row that is not observable here is `strtotime`'s ambient-zone dependence itself: two runs under two zones is two processes, so what the case can assert is that no such state is read, which it does by refusing the text that would have needed it.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Iso {
    // A refusal names the class it is refused as, so a member that grew a
    // second failure mode fails this row rather than reading as one refusal.
    public static function of(string $text): string {
        try {
            return Core\Time::fromIso($text)->toEpochSeconds() as string;
        } catch (ParseError $e) {
            return "ParseError";
        } catch (Throwable $other) {
            return "other";
        }
    }
}

var $utc = Core\Time\Zone::UTC;
uint $refused = 0;

// A civil time with no offset: PHP places it in the ambient zone, `fromIso`
// refuses it, and `Core\Time::parse` is the member that names the zone.
echo "date-only|", Iso::of("2024-02-29"), "\n";
echo "no-offset|", Iso::of("2024-02-29T13:45:07"), "\n";
echo Core\Time::parse("2024-02-29 13:45:07", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss", $utc)->toInstant()->toEpochSeconds(), "\n";

// A relative expression, which is arithmetic against the clock rather than a
// parse. Written out, its other operand is a value the caller chose.
echo "relative-day|", Iso::of("+1 day"), "\n";
echo "now|", Iso::of("now"), "\n";
echo "weekday|", Iso::of("next Thursday"), "\n";
echo Core\Time::fromIso("2024-02-29T13:45:07Z")->in($utc)->plus(1, Core\Unit::Day)->toInstant()->toEpochSeconds(), "\n";
echo Core\Time::fromIso("2024-02-29T13:45:07Z")->in($utc)->next(Core\Weekday::Thursday)->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";

// A text that spells nothing at all, where `strtotime` answers `false` — `0`
// in every numeric position it is then used in.
echo "garbage|", Iso::of("not a date"), "\n";
echo "empty|", Iso::of(""), "\n";

// The day February does not have, which PHP normalizes into the next month.
echo "rolled-over|", Iso::of("2024-02-30T00:00:00Z"), "\n";

// Counted rather than read off the lines above.
array<string> $guesses = ["2024-02-29", "2024-02-29T13:45:07", "+1 day", "now", "next Thursday", "not a date", "", "2024-02-30T00:00:00Z"];
foreach ($guesses as string $text) {
    if (Iso::of($text) == "ParseError") { $refused = $refused + 1; }
}
echo "refused=", $refused, "/8\n";
--EXPECT--
date-only|ParseError
no-offset|ParseError
1709214307
relative-day|ParseError
now|ParseError
weekday|ParseError
1709300707
2024-03-07
garbage|ParseError
empty|ParseError
rolled-over|ParseError
refused=8/8
